<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahuitzotl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1486">1486</a>–<a href="/years/1502">1502</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahuitzotl was the eighth tlatoani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, reigning from 1486 to 1502. He led extensive military campaigns that expanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> to its greatest extent, reaching as far as the Pacific coast and areas near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. He also oversaw the rededication of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> in 1487.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl.jpg" alt="Ahuitzotl" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahuitzotl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ahuitzotl was the eighth Aztec ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huey-tlatoani">Huey Tlatoani</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, and a son of the princess Atotoztli II. His name literally means Water Thorny and was also applied to the otter, though it is theorised that the animal called ahuitzotl is more likely the water opossum, the hand symbolising its prehensile tail. Either he or his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tizoc">Tizoc</a> was the first tlatoani of Tenochtitlan to take the title Huey Tlatoani, meaning supreme tlatoani, to make clear its superiority over the other cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a>. He took power in the year 7 Rabbit, 1486, after the death of his brother Tizoc.</p><p>Perhaps the foremost known military leader of pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, he began his reign by suppressing a Huastec rebellion and then swiftly more than doubled the lands under Aztec dominance. He conquered the Mixtec, Zapotec and other peoples from the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> down to the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. He also supervised a major rebuilding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> on a grander scale, including the expansion of the Great Pyramid, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a>, in the year 8 Reed, 1487. During his time the great-tailed grackle was introduced into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, the earliest documented case of a human-mediated bird introduction in the Western Hemisphere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ahuitzotl died in the year 10 Rabbit, 1502, and was succeeded by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>. He had two sons, the kings Chimalpilli II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a>, and one daughter. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> took his name from the animal, which they considered a creature of myth in its own right rather than a mere mythical representation of the king.</p><p>He is commemorated by one of the bronze statues of the Monumento a los Indios Verdes in Gustavo A. Madero, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, which was originally made for the 1889 Paris Exposition. He appears as a character under the name Teomitl in Aliette de Bodard's Obsidian and Blood series, and prominently in Gary Jennings's historical novel Aztec, which recounts his expansions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and his wars of conquest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahuitzotl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ahuitzotl</b> (r. 1486–1502), the eighth Huey Tlatoani, was the greatest Aztec conqueror.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Ahuitzotl</b> (r. 1486–1502) the Aztecs renewed the pressure through allied peoples of the Balsas region, but King Zuangua held the frontier, and the Aztecs never conquered the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aztec Empire under <b>Ahuitzotl</b> sought to seize the isthmus, but Cocijoeza held out at the mountain fortress of Guiengola in a long siege in the late 1490s.</blockquote><a href="/state/zapotec-kingdom-of-tehuantepec">Zapotec Kingdom of Tehuantepec</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1494" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1872 1444 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahuitzotl"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2322 1714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1494">What was here in 1494? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
